<style>
  /* Give trmnlp's preview a device profile, without overriding a real one.
     ---------------------------------------------------------------
     TRMNL's framework carries a per-device block — `.screen--ogv2`,
     `.screen--v2`, `.screen--amazon_kindle_2024` — setting that
     hardware's screen size, pixel ratio, gap scale and colour depth on
     `.screen`. TRMNL stamps the right class on the wrapper when it
     renders for a device.

     `trmnlp` does not. It emits a bare `class="screen
     screen--fonts-trmnl"`, so a local build falls back to the `:root`
     defaults and renders a board no device will ever show. A `<style>`
     here cannot add a class to that wrapper, but it can set the same
     variables the class would — before trmnlp takes its screenshot, so
     the PNG gets them too.

     **The layer is what makes this safe.** The framework declares
     `@layer tn--normalize, tn--elements, tn--components, tn--base,
     tn--device-overrides, tn--themes, tn--utilities` and puts every
     device block in `tn--base`. Unlayered CSS beats all of them, so an
     unlayered copy of one device's numbers wins on real hardware too:
     a TRMNL X asks for 1040x780, gets 800x480, and draws the board in
     the corner of its panel. Declaring these in `tn--normalize` — the
     first layer, so the weakest — inverts that. trmnlp, which stamps
     no device class, has nothing to override it and renders the OG.
     A device whose class is present wins, and renders itself.

     Values copied verbatim from `.trmnl .screen--ogv2` in
     https://trmnl.com/css/3.4.4/plugins.css. `tests/layouts.test.ts`
     checks they still match, because a framework upgrade that retunes
     a device would otherwise drift silently.

     **--quadrant-w/h are deliberately not copied.** The device sets
     them to 100%, but that resolves against trmnlp's own unsized
     wrapper and collapses the quadrant view to an empty title bar —
     verified by rendering it. They are the one pair whose device value
     depends on markup trmnlp does not reproduce, so the fallback
     arithmetic is what keeps the local quadrant readable.

     The *other* half of matching the device is --modifier-scale, which
     is a per-plugin user setting (screen--scale-small .. xxlarge) and
     not a device property. It is left alone deliberately: the default
     of 1 is scale Regular. If the panel is set to anything else, the
     preview will not match and this block is not the place to fix it. */
  @layer tn--normalize {
    .trmnl .screen {
      --screen-w: 800px;
      --screen-h: 480px;
      --screen-w-original: 800px;
      --screen-h-original: 480px;
      --pixel-ratio: 1;
      --dither-pixel-ratio: 1;
      --device-ui-scale: 1;
      --gap-scale: 1;
      --color-depth: 2;
      --density-tier: 1x;
      --device-name: "ogv2";
    }
  }
  /* Scale the TRMNL X's type and spacing to its larger canvas.
     ---------------------------------------------------------------
     Both device blocks set `--device-ui-scale: 1`, so every type size —
     each one `calc(<px> * var(--text-ui-scale))`, where `--text-ui-scale`
     is `--device-ui-scale * --modifier-scale * --modifier-text-scale` —
     comes out identical on both devices. The X's canvas is 1040x780 CSS
     pixels against the OG's 800x480: 2.1x the area drawn at the same type
     size, which is the "mostly content, not whitespace" check TRMNL's
     reviewers apply.

     One variable moves all of it because the layouts size everything
     downstream in `em`: bullets at 1.35em, row padding at 0.1/0.3em, row
     margins at 0.14em. Raising the scale grows glyphs, badges and the
     space around them together, in proportion, with no per-element list
     to keep in step.

     **In `tn--device-overrides`, which beats `tn--base`.** That is the
     opposite of the pin above, and deliberately so: the pin supplies a
     profile only where none exists and must lose to a real device, while
     this one corrects a value the device itself sets and must win. The
     framework declares the layer immediately after `tn--base` and uses it
     for one 2-bit title-bar rule, so nothing here competes.

     `--modifier-scale` and `--modifier-text-scale` are untouched: those
     carry the user's own size choice, and multiply with this rather than
     being overridden by it, so a panel set to Large still steps up from
     here.

     1.25 is the 1040/800 width ratio, rounded down to a value that
     divides cleanly. Type and row pitch grow together, so a larger scale
     buys legibility and costs rows: measured against `panel-height`'s
     budget, a full column on an X draws 23 rows at scale 1 and 18 at
     1.25, and a split column 11 against 8.

     1.3 yields the same 18 and 8 — the row count is a floor division, so
     it steps rather than slides — and packs them marginally tighter (558
     of the 561px budget against 540). The extra 5% was not taken because
     the rows it would enlarge are already the width-critical ones: a
     split subway column clips its destination at scale 1, and every
     increment here clips it further. 1.25 is the smaller of two steps
     that cost the same rows.

     `row_pitch` in `panel-height` is measured at this scale. The two are
     one tuning and must move together. */
  @layer tn--device-overrides {
    .trmnl .screen--v2 {
      --device-ui-scale: 1.25;
    }
  }
</style>
